{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And the name of his mother.</b> One needs to know why Scripture did not mention her name at the outset, when it mentioned him up until his being brought to Moses. And one may say in two ways: the first, to praise — <b>they brought him to Moses, and the name of his mother</b> etc., meaning, she too was among those who brought him. And the other, to disparage — <b>they brought him to Moses</b>, and through this act of bringing she became liable to have Scripture publicize her, that his mother was Shelomit, and from the implication of the matter you know that her compassion was stirred for her son, and it is written (Proverbs 12:10) ״וְרַחֲמֵי רְשָׁעִים אַכְזָרִי״ — therefore Scripture disclosed her, that this woman and this her son. Scripture also mentioned her tribe for the reason I have written myself, for from the tribe's protective silence a tribe will feel compassion and stand to aid its kin, and therefore they said in the Midrash (Leviticus Rabbah 32): disgrace to him, disgrace to his tribe, etc."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:41.930191537Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}